WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:04.061
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome back to the
show today, Cal Newport, who's a computer

00:00:04.061 --> 00:00:09.350
science professor at Georgetown University,
author of several books and most recently,

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:14.530
which I just finished, a world without email,
reimagining work in an age of communication

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:17.130
overload. Karl, it's always great to have
you on.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:22.210
Speaker 3: Yeah, I'm happy to be back.
Speaker 1: So in the book, A World Without

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:28.789
Email, one of the things you explain is that
many jobs, even jobs that don't really have

00:00:28.789 --> 00:00:36.420
to do with email in terms of what the main
impetus of the job is, have become 20, 30,

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:41.020
40, sometimes even more than half, managing
an inbox, responding to emails in a way that

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:46.760
is anywhere from relatively to completely
unproductive. You talk about the history of

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:51.850
this, and it's really interesting because
when email was introduced to office culture,

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:57.650
you didn't just have office culture and work
culture as it existed with the addition of

00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:03.079
email. It completely changed the way that
work worked, so to speak. Can you talk a little

00:01:03.079 --> 00:01:07.000
bit about that first?
Speaker 3: This is what caught us off guard

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.580
in the world of work that when we brought
email into our offices, which we did predominantly

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:15.580
in the first half of the nineteen nineties,
that's when it really spread aggressively

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:20.760
through offices. We did so for very narrow,
pragmatic reasons. We were replacing fax machines.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:26.590
We were replacing voicemail. We were replacing
memos like, great, this tool is better at

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:32.370
those purposes than the existing technology
we were using once email actually got into

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:37.560
office as though it had an unexpected side
effect, which is people began using it as

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:42.470
the primary means by which they coordinated
or collaborated. We introduced a workflow

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:47.740
in which everything began to be worked out
with back and forth informal ad hoc messaging

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:53.490
that didn't scale. And soon we found ourselves
overwhelmed with all of the back and forth

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:57.000
messages required to support this workflow
where everything happens through back and

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.580
forth messages. And so really that workflow,
not the tool itself, not the way that it replaced

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:05.100
fax machines, not the way replace voice mail,
but this workflow where we began to work everything

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:08.590
out with back and forth messages, that's what
created the overload problem that we're all

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:12.170
suffering from today.
Speaker 1: To some degree, it seems almost

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:18.680
like it's become background in this particular
sense. My mom also is a college professor

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:22.110
and she's been teaching for decades at this
point. And I asked her in preparation for

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:27.220
this interview, I'd go back like in the in
the 90s before you were so dependent on email

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:31.990
for communication with your department and
communication with students and so on. What

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:35.860
consumed your day? Because roughly you're
working the same amount of time, but now a

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:40.959
ton of the time is email. Clearly, the email
time now was for something else before she

00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:45.760
had no idea, like in other words, it's sort
of crept in in such a way where she agrees.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:50.720
I work roughly the same amount. I didn't used
to do the email thing. Now I do. I don't remember

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:55.220
what I did before. Is is that surprising to
you or does that make sense?

00:02:55.220 --> 00:03:00.590
Speaker 3: Well, I think we should not underestimate
the degree to which this workflow in which

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:05.820
now we have to tend these inboxes all day
is really actually putting its hand down firmly

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:11.530
on economic growth. And looking at a college
professor is a it's a good bottle case study

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:15.730
because it's a it's a job that has been basically
the same for two hundred years. And in my

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:21.520
book I talked about my grandfather was the
great example, a very successful professor,

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:26.110
endowed chair at Rice University that went
on to be the provost of a theological seminary,

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:30.739
incredibly productive, such an effective teacher
that even to this day I still hear from his

00:03:30.739 --> 00:03:35.540
former students. He had such a big impact
on his life. He never owned a computer until

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:39.959
after he retired. I'm thinking his objectives
are no different than my objectives as a professor

00:03:39.959 --> 00:03:44.670
today to do research and the teach well, and
he had no email. And yet today we'll spend

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:48.830
like your your mom was talking about 50 to
60 percent of our time on email. It's a real

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:53.420
issue. So what is getting squeezed out here?
I think productive output. I think the reason

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:57.560
why non-industrial productivity metrics have
been stagnant for decades, even as we put

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:02.819
all of this innovation into very rapid, low
friction, communication and computation, technology

00:04:02.819 --> 00:04:06.940
is because we are moving more and more of
our time and extending this communication.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:10.960
And I think what we have to do to compensate
for that is we're adding extra work off the

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:15.200
books. We're working early in the morning.
We're working in the evening. We're basically

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:19.650
trying to just keep productivity stable by
adding a lot more hours into the mix. It's

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:25.370
a huge issue and it's one I think we're ignoring
Speaker 1: one approach to the issue. And

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:29.220
some of your past writing has sort of dealt
with this. And there's many other people who

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:36.440
write about this is about techniques and strategies,
train others to expect that you check email

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:41.340
once a day or at the beginning, at the end
or three times a week or whatever. And over

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:47.900
time, that's going to help everybody or write
your emails in a way where you are providing

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:53.900
the other person everything they need to complete
the task rather than communicating the minimum,

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:57.370
which then creates a back and forth over a
week for something that a five minute phone

00:04:57.370 --> 00:05:01.900
call might be able to fix. That's one approach
like the very nitty gritty logistical approach

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:06.360
that that doesn't seem to me like it really
gets at the bigger picture issue. What do

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:11.460
you think that we need to be tackling both
the big picture and the nitty gritty, or could

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:14.711
the nitty gritty really solve a lot of the
problem?

00:05:14.711 --> 00:05:17.860
Speaker 3: The nitty gritty alone is not going
to solve the problem. And this is why I think

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:23.100
we're stuck as we've been trying to solve
the issue of email overload in the inbox itself.

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:27.840
Oh, spend less time checking emails back.
Your emails have better subject lines, right?

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:31.780
Have better norms, have better expectations,
but we can't solve this problem. This is one

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:35.130
of the sort of the big idea of the new book
is we can't solve this problem just in the

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:41.010
inbox. The issue is the underlying workflows
so long as implicitly the way that your organization

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:45.449
collaborates is through ad hoc, back and forth
unscheduled messages that a man to keep up

00:05:45.449 --> 00:05:49.550
with all of these back and forth threads demands
that you check your inbox all the time because

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:53.890
that is how your work is actually happening.
This is why when we don't change that underlying

00:05:53.890 --> 00:05:58.520
workflow but just say have better habits when
you personally interact with your inbox, that

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:05.110
fails. So my big push now is to get past this
this moment we're in, which we're. Remley

00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:09.210
subduing our productivity because of all the
constant contact, shifting communication,

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:13.039
we have to fix the underlying workflows, that
is the rules and the guidelines and the agreements

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:17.919
we have about how collaboration happens. It
can't just be rock and roll and email, rock

00:06:17.919 --> 00:06:21.699
and roll and slack rock and roll teams will
just figure it out. That does not work. It

00:06:21.699 --> 00:06:25.700
is a bad way to build the metaphorical cars,
if you know what I mean. And so we have to

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:29.680
fix this at that level before the messages
get into the inbox in the first place.

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:34.009
Speaker 1: In the book, you talk about fixing
it within an organization in terms of the

00:06:34.009 --> 00:06:40.230
communication with coworkers, and it's sort
of different at least than fixing it when

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:45.259
it comes to people you interface outside of
your organization, like, for example, consultants

00:06:45.259 --> 00:06:50.419
working with clients, which include examples
of in the book. Does it make sense to first

00:06:50.419 --> 00:06:56.830
fix it internally before doing it externally?
Speaker 3: Both of these can happen in parallel.

00:06:56.830 --> 00:07:01.990
It doesn't quite matter. What really matters
is having that mindset shift, which is saying,

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:05.370
OK, our work is made up of processes. We might
not call it that, but that's what they are,

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:09.509
the things we do on a regular basis that produce
the value for our team, our organization,

00:07:09.509 --> 00:07:13.460
our company. OK, how are we implementing these
processes? How do we get the information?

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:17.580
Where do we store the information? How do
we communicate with each other to actually

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:21.690
accomplish these processes? What are our rules
for that? And if you don't have rules written

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:25.460
down, the default answer is probably this
hyperactive hive mind approach. We'll just

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:29.400
work it out on the fly once you have that
mindset. Yeah. You're going to see those client

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:33.419
communication processes. Let's make those
betters their internal processes. How do we

00:07:33.419 --> 00:07:37.600
schedule meetings? How do we agree on new
copy for ads? You fix those processes. But

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:42.840
the big step here is that mindset shift that
even in knowledge work, there's things you

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:47.080
do again and again. And either you have rules
about how you want to collaborate to accomplish

00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:50.639
those things or you don't. And if you don't,
you're probably leaving a huge amount of productivity

00:07:50.639 --> 00:07:52.690
on the table.
Speaker 1: One of the things you talk about

