00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:16.569
company. This really wouldn't work. And and
that belief seems to be a very prevalent.

00:08:16.569 --> 00:08:22.699
Can you talk a little bit more specifically
about the types of changes that probably would

00:08:22.699 --> 00:08:27.240
work in just about any knowledge work field?
Speaker 3: Well, I think one of the problems

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:32.479
here is that when people think about reforming
email, they think about, OK, what if nothing

00:08:32.479 --> 00:08:35.699
changed about the underlying workflow and
they're imagining it. They're imagining their

00:08:35.699 --> 00:08:39.599
work as it happens right now under organization
where most things are figured out with back

00:08:39.599 --> 00:08:44.249
and forth messaging. And then they say in
that context, if I stopped using email, as

00:08:44.249 --> 00:08:48.600
much bad things would happen. And they're
right. If your organization or team uses this

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:52.670
hyperactive hive mind workflow, it would be
a problem if you stopped using it. So getting

00:08:52.670 --> 00:08:56.910
people to look below the inbox and say, no,
no, no, we want to fix how you collaborate

00:08:56.910 --> 00:09:00.990
in the first place, that those messages don't
show up in your inbox, that you're not figuring

00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:04.779
things out with unscheduled messages and we
get down to the roots of it. So what are those

00:09:04.779 --> 00:09:09.360
changes look like? It depends on the process,
right. So this is why this is not a it's not

